course: books
lesson: 47
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VI, part 3: Mr. Toad (the doctor, the lawyer, and the rope of sheets)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: One fine morning it's the Rat's turn to guard Toad. The Badger warns him at the door: when Toad is quiet and meek, playing the good little hero of a Sunday-school prize book, that's when he is at his trickiest. «There's sure to be something up.»
explain: Toad lies flat in bed and talks in a weak little voice: so kind of dear Ratty to ask, but he doesn't want to be a burden… he won't be one much longer… Would Ratty perhaps fetch the doctor? But don't bother — it's only a trouble. And, while he's at it, would he ask the lawyer to come too?
explain: A lawyer! The Rat is alarmed — Toad must be really ill. He decides to be on the safe side, carefully locks the door, and runs off to the village. The moment the key turns, Toad hops lightly out of bed, puts on his smartest suit, fills his pockets with money from a drawer, ties his bed-sheets into a rope, climbs out of the window, and marches off the other way, whistling a merry tune.
explain: At lunch the poor Rat has to tell the Badger and the Mole. The Badger says some very sharp things; even the Mole says, «You've been a bit of a duffer this time, Ratty!» «He did it awfully well,» says the Rat. «He did you awfully well!» snaps the Badger. They'll keep sleeping at Toad Hall: Toad may be brought back any moment — on a stretcher, or between two policemen.
fact: The Badger was «fidgeting to be off and stretch his legs in a long ramble round his wood and down his earths and burrows» (earths = burrows, homes underground).
fact: Toad, says the Badger, keeps saying «O leave him alone, he wants nothing, perhaps he'll be better presently, it may pass off in time, don't be unduly anxious».
fact: The Badger's warning: «When Toad's quiet and submissive and playing at being the hero of a Sunday-school prize, then he's at his artfullest. There's sure to be something up. I know him.» (Submissive = obeying meekly; artful = cunning.)
fact: The Rat «incautiously» (carelessly) tells Toad that the Mole and the Badger «will be out till luncheon time» — so Toad knows he'll be alone with the Rat.
fact: Toad's «feeble voice»: «Thank you so much, dear Ratty! So good of you to inquire! But first tell me how you are yourself, and the excellent Mole?»
fact: «I hate being a burden to my friends, and I do not expect to be one much longer. Indeed, I almost hope not.» The Rat, missing the hint: «Well, I hope not, too … You've been a fine bother to us all this time».
fact: Toad: «I'm afraid it is the trouble you mind … I'm a nuisance, I know.» The Rat: «You are, indeed. But I tell you, I'd take any trouble on earth for you, if only you'd be a sensible animal.»
fact: «Fetch the doctor. But don't you bother. It's only a trouble» — Toad keeps saying «don't bother», which makes the Rat bother all the more.
fact: «Surely you have noticed of late——» … «To-morrow, indeed, you may be saying to yourself, ‘O, if only I had noticed sooner!’» — he wants the Rat to feel guilty.
fact: Toad asks for «the lawyer» too: «there are moments—perhaps I should say there is a moment—when one must face disagreeable tasks» (as if he were dying and needed to make a will).
fact: The Rat: «It's best to be on the safe side … I've never heard him ask for a lawyer! If there's nothing really the matter, the doctor will tell him he's an old ass». He locks the door carefully and goes.
fact: Toad «hopped lightly out of bed», dressed «in the smartest suit he could lay hands on», took «cash … from a small drawer in the dressing-table», knotted his sheets into «the improvised rope» (improvised = made up on the spot), tied it to «the central mullion of the handsome Tudor window» (mullion = the stone bar dividing a window), and «slid lightly to the ground».
fact: He took «the opposite direction to the Rat» and «marched off lightheartedly, whistling a merry tune».
fact: Mole: «You've been a bit of a duffer this time, Ratty! Toad, too, of all animals!» (duffer = a silly, clumsy person.) Rat: «He did it awfully well.» Badger: «He did you awfully well!» (did you = tricked you.)
fact: The Badger fears Toad will be so proud of his clever trick that he may do any silly thing now.
fact: The Badger: «Toad may be brought back at any moment—on a stretcher, or between two policemen.» The writer warns that «much water, and of how turbid a character» (turbid = muddy) would run under bridges before Toad sat at ease in his Hall again — lots of trouble lies ahead.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «sus» = suspicious; «sussed out» (UK) = saw through, worked out; «ken» (Scotland) = know; «dinnae» (Scotland) = don't; «wee» (Scotland) = small.
exercise: Whose turn is it to guard Toad this morning? | The Rat's | The Mole's, as on every Monday | The Badger's | The Otter's
exercise: Where is the Badger off to? | A long ramble round his wood and burrows | The village, to fetch the doctor himself | The river | Bed
exercise: When is Toad «at his artfullest», says the Badger? | When he's quiet, meek and acting like a goody-goody | When he's shouting rude names through the keyhole at the guards | When he's asleep | When he's eating
exercise: What does the Rat carelessly tell Toad? | The Mole and the Badger will be out till lunch | The spare key of the bedroom is hidden under the doormat outside | The car is outside | Nothing at all
exercise: How does Toad speak to the Rat? | In a feeble voice, all thanks and politeness | In a loud, angry shout from under the bed, kicking the floor | Cheerfully | Not at all
exercise: «I do not expect to be one much longer.» What is Toad hinting? | That he may soon die | That he is going on holiday next week | That he'll buy a car | That he'll get married
exercise: What does the Rat say he'd do «if only you'd be a sensible animal»? | Take any trouble on earth for him | Give him back his motor-clothes and goggles | Leave Toad Hall | Buy him a boat
exercise: Whom does Toad first ask the Rat to fetch? | The doctor | The Badger, quickly | The police, at once | His old housekeeper
exercise: Why does Toad keep saying «don't bother»? | To make the Rat bother all the more | Because he truly doesn't want a doctor at all | He is too weak | He is polite
exercise: Whom else does Toad ask for, «while you are about it»? | The lawyer | The vicar from the village church | The chauffeur | The Mole, back from the wood
exercise: Why does the lawyer frighten the Rat so much? | People call for a lawyer when they think they're dying | Lawyers are always very rude and expensive, and the Rat hates them | The Rat owes the lawyer money | The lawyer is a weasel
exercise: What does the Rat do before he leaves the room? | Locks the door carefully | Gives Toad a hot drink | Opens the window to let in air | Leaves the door open
exercise: «If there's nothing really the matter, the doctor will tell him…» what? | He's an old ass, and cheer him up | He must stay in bed for a whole year | He must sell his cars | He must leave
exercise: When does Toad hop out of bed? | As soon as he hears the key turn | After an hour, when it's quite safe | At night, when everyone is asleep in bed | When the doctor comes up the stairs
exercise: What does Toad put on? | The smartest suit he could find | His goggles, cap, gaiters and enormous overcoat | His dressing-gown | A disguise
exercise: Where does Toad get money? | A small drawer in his dressing-table | The Rat's coat pocket, hanging on the door | The bank in the town, near the station | The Badger's coat, left on the hall chair
exercise: How does Toad get out? | Down a rope of knotted bed-sheets from the window | Through the door, with a spare key he had hidden in his slipper | Down the chimney | Through a tunnel
exercise: What does he tie the rope to? | The central mullion of the Tudor window | The leg of his heavy bed | A tree | The door-handle
exercise: Which way does Toad go? | The opposite way to the Rat | The same way, close behind him | Down to the river to find a boat | Back up to bed by the back stairs
exercise: How does Toad feel walking off? | Lighthearted, whistling a merry tune | Frightened, looking back over his shoulder all the time | Sad, and sorry for tricking the Rat | Ill and weak, just as he told the Rat
exercise: What does the Mole call the Rat at lunch? | A bit of a duffer | A wicked, careless traitor to his friends | A real hero, very brave | A greedy, selfish pig
exercise: «He did you awfully well!» What does the Badger mean? | Toad tricked you very cleverly | Toad treated you very kindly | Toad did a good job | Toad thanked you
exercise: Why does the Badger fear Toad will do something silly now? | He'll be so proud of his trick he may do anything | He has no money left at all and is very hungry and tired | He is ill | He is lost
exercise: How might Toad be brought back, says the Badger? | On a stretcher, or between two policemen | In his own new motor-car, driving very fast | By boat | By train
test: Leah: «the Rat sussed Toad out straight away.» (sussed out = saw through) Put it right. | He was fooled, and ran for the doctor and lawyer | Right: he saw the trick at once and stayed by the door | He never went in | The Mole saw through it
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Badger had warned the Rat that very morning.» | True: «There's sure to be something up. I know him.» | False: the Badger had no idea Toad would try anything | False, he was asleep | True, but about the weasels
test: Hamish from Dundee: «Toad's no really ill, ken.» (ken = you know) What clues showed he was only pretending? | He kept saying «don't bother», hinting, and asked for a lawyer too | He coughed very loudly all morning and had a very high fever | He had spots | There were no clues at all
test: «You may be saying to yourself, ‘O, if only I had noticed sooner!’» What is Toad trying to do to the Rat? | Make him feel guilty so he'll go | Make him laugh at a joke about the doctor | Thank him | Tell him the truth
test: Toad says, «Noticing things is only a trouble.» Why does he say it? | To make the Rat feel bad for not noticing | Because he hates noticing things and wants to sleep | Because he is tired | He means the Rat is wise
test: Why did the Rat lock the door even though he believed Toad was ill? | He still followed the rule never to leave Toad free | He wanted to keep the doctor out of the room until he came back | He was angry with Toad | He forgot why
test: Looking back: in the last lesson, the Rat said «Told you so» about Toad. What happens to the Rat now? | He gets tricked by Toad himself | He tricks Toad back | He leaves for the river | He tells the Badger «I told you so» again
test: Looking back: in chapter II, why did the Rat say they couldn't leave Toad on the trip? | «It wouldn't be safe for him to be left to himself» | «He'd steal the horse and ride off alone» | «He'd eat all the food» | «He'd be lonely»
test: Looking back: in chapter V, the Mole cried real tears about his home. How are Toad's tears different? | Toad's tears and weak voice are an act to get his way | Toad's tears are real and much worse than the Mole's ever were | Toad never cries | They are the same
test: Looking back: in chapter III the Rat worked out the door-scraper; in chapter V the sardines. What's different this time? | Now the clever Rat is fooled | Now the Rat is even cleverer | Now the Mole is fooled | Nothing is different
test: Looking back: which escape does Toad's rope of sheets remind you of in this book so far? | None — it's the first escape out of a window in the book | The Mole's escape from the Wild Wood by the window | The Otter's escape from the weasels | The Rat's escape down a rope in chapter I
test: Looking back: in chapter II the Rat said Toad was «always glad to see you, always sorry when you go!» How is that turned upside down now? | Now Toad wants his friend to go, so that he can escape | Now Toad is sorry to see the Rat arrive, and cries until he leaves | Toad is never glad to see anyone at all, even in chapter II | Nothing has changed
test: Kirsty, lowkey sus: «wait, if Badger knew, why'd he leave the Rat alone?» (sus = suspicious) What's the best answer from the text? | He did warn the Rat — but the Rat still got fooled | The Badger secretly wanted Toad to escape and get it over with | He didn't know Toad at all | He was asleep
test: What would have happened if the Rat had asked the Badger before running off? | The Badger would probably have seen through Toad's trick | The Badger would have run for the doctor and the lawyer too, even faster | Toad would be cured | Nothing different
test: ★★★ «When Toad's quiet and submissive … then he's at his artfullest.» Which of Toad's words that morning best fits «submissive»? | «I hate being a burden to my friends» | «Shan't!» | «Poop-poop!» | «It was simply glorious!»
test: ★★★ «He did it awfully well.» — «He did you awfully well!» What changes the meaning? | Adding «you» turns «did it» (acted) into «did you» (tricked you) | Nothing at all, the two sentences mean just the same | «Awfully» means «badly» | «Did» means «stole»
test: ★★★ «Much water, and of how turbid a character, was to run under bridges.» What is the writer telling us? | A lot of troubled time will pass before Toad is home again | It is going to rain very hard for many days and nights | The river will flood | Toad will fall into the river
exam: Identify the warning conveyed by the Badger to the Rat upon the morning of Toad's escape. | That Toad was most cunning when most submissive | That Toad was dangerously ill and required a physician | That the weasels were near | No warning was given
exam: Ascertain the persons whose attendance Toad requested of the Rat. | A doctor and a lawyer | A doctor and a nurse, with medicines from the village apothecary | The Badger and the Mole | The police
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means of Toad's escape from his bedroom? | A rope of knotted sheets secured to the mullion of the window | A duplicate key concealed beneath the mattress for the purpose | The chimney | The door, left unlocked
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Badger's arrangements following the escape. | Continued residence at Toad Hall, against Toad's possible return | Immediate pursuit of Toad by all three animals along the high road | Departure for the Wild Wood | Notification of the police
